{
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "score": 7,
  "estimated_minutes": 60,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must understand Wayne’s challenge to Commission approval authority under Section 3.8, but the supplied concurrence does not identify Wayne’s proposed activity or either party’s precise interpretation."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The interstate Compact’s allocation of water-resource authority supplies relevant background, although the concurrence identifies no technical industry knowledge needed to understand “project.”"
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding “project” requires reading Section 3.8 alongside the detailed definition in Section 1.2(g), including its references to work, services, activities, facilities and water-resource purposes."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "The supplied opinion is a concurrence that confirms ambiguity and remand but refers to an absent majority opinion for the competing constructions and expressly declines to evaluate them."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The identified defect concerns only the meaning of “project,” which is readily distinguished from Section 3.8’s separate substantial-effect and comprehensive-plan requirements."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d01 — The ambiguity holding is explicit, but the supplied concurrence never states Wayne’s or the Commission’s interpretation of “project.” The reviewer needs the majority opinion to understand those readings and distinguish the ambiguity actually found from arguments left for remand.",
  "reason": "The basic procedural result is clear: “project” is ambiguous, neither party’s interpretation has been adopted, and the case returns for fact-finding about the drafters’ intent. Reading Sections 3.8 and 1.2(g) explains the approval mechanism but does not establish what activities the parties disputed or how their constructions differed. Assign this to a reviewer comfortable with interstate compacts and identifying the limits of an appellate holding, and obtain the referenced majority opinion before expecting reliable matching of paraphrased defects. The estimate allows for that targeted additional reading; the supplied concurrence alone cannot support the full understanding required."
}
